BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN HARRIET ALIDA LYE IN CONVERSATION WITH ASHLEY AUDRAIN
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260610T181500
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260610T200000
UID:bm-5435020260610@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/Motherclo
 wn_Flying_Books_Event___June_10__v2___1_.jpeg?cb=1779367686
DESCRIPTION:Join Flying Books and McClelland & Stewart for an evening of c
 onversation with Harriet Alida Lye as she discusses her new book\nMothercl
 own\nwith Ashley Audrain (bestselling author of\nThe Push\n).\nAshley also
  wrote the new introduction to the reissue of Harriet's memoir\nNatural Ki
 ller\n\, which will be released alongside\nMotherclown.\nMotherclown\nexpl
 ores the most fundamental and potentially explosive of human relationships
 : that of a mother and her daughter.\n\nIn the wake of her father’s deat
 h\, twenty-one-year-old Elise leaves her hometown of Niagara Falls to move
  to Paris\, where she begins training at Chevalier\, an elite physical the
 atre school focused on clown.\n\nLeft behind\, Elise’s mother Catherine 
 is unravelling—grieving\, restless\, and haunted by regret. When Elise d
 isappears into Paris\, Catherine follows.\n\nAs Paris works its spell on t
 hem both\, old wounds surface and resentments ignite. Can Elise hear her m
 other without losing herself? Can Catherine face what she has done—or is
  she there to swallow her daughter whole?\n\nMotherclown is an electric no
 vel about art and inheritance\, desire and devotion\, and the ferocious\, 
 vulnerable love between mothers and daughters.\nHARRIET ALIDA LYE is the a
 ward-winning author of the novels\nLet It Destroy You\nand\nThe Honey Farm
 \,\nthe memoir\nNatural Killer\n\, and co-author of the picture book S\ner
 ge the Snail Without a Shell.\nHer work has been published in\nThe New Yor
 k Times\, The Globe and Mail\, National Post\, The Happy Reader\, Hazlitt\
 , Vice\, Catapult\n\, and more. She lives in Toronto.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College Street
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Celebrate the launch of Nowhere with Jon Claytor and special guest
 s\, Ian Roy and José Contreras!
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260611T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260611T200000
UID:bm-5151120260611@flyingbooks.ca
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nCome and celebrate the launch of\nNowhere\nwith
  Jon Claytor and special guests\, Ian Roy and José Contreras! This event 
 is free and open to the public.\nA hilarious and sinister reflection on gr
 owing up in a world where monsters are real\, Nowhere is a dreamlike medit
 ation on adolescence and the absurdity of small-town life.\n \nJon will be
  reading from this unforgettable graphic novel\, Ian will be reading from 
 his brand new short story collection\,\nAstrid\, Aghast\n\, and singer-son
 gwriter José will play a short set and musical accompaniment to the eveni
 ng’s readings.\nAbout the Authors:\nJON CLAYTOR is a graphic novelist\, 
 painter\, and writer. Born in San Francisco\, he lived and worked in sever
 al Canadian cities\, including Halifax\, Vancouver\, Montreal\, and Toront
 o\, before settling in Sackville\, New Brunswick.\nClaytor’s work ranges
  from oil painting and watercolour to comics. He has had solo exhibitions 
 at galleries in Montreal\, Toronto\, Los Angeles\, and throughout the Mari
 times. He has also written and illustrated stories for CBC Radio\, the Fry
 e Festival\, and the website ruralharmreduction.com.\nIAN ROY is a writer\
 , screen printer\, and educator. His work has appeared in numerous magazin
 es and journals\, including\nThe Antigonish Review\, Arc\, Geist\, Long Co
 n Magazine\, Maisonneuve\,\nand\nThe New Quarterly.\nIan is the author of 
 six books\, including the short story collection\,\nPeople Leaving\,\nwhic
 h was short-listed for both the City of Ottawa Book Award and the Upper Ca
 nada Writers’ Craft Award.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College St.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR OUR JUNE FICTION BOOK CLUB:
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260622T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260623T203000
UID:bm-5435920260622@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/June_Book
 club_.png?cb=1779283324
DESCRIPTION:[Book Club]\nJoin us for a discussion of the novel\,\nSisters 
 in Yellow\nby one of the great writers of her generation\, Mieko Kawakami\
 nFrom the award-winning author of\nBreasts and Eggs\,\ncomes a bold novel 
 of sacrifice and the tumultuous bonds of sisterhood\, set in the gritty To
 kyo of the 1990s.\n\nHana is fifteen years old\, lives with her mother\, h
 as no money\, and no security. Kimiko is older\, a bright light in Hana’
 s dark world. Together they set up a bar. Suddenly Hana has a job she love
 s\, friends to share her days with\, and the glittering promise of money. 
 She feels like a normal girl.\nBut in the narrow alleys of Sangenjaya\, no
 thing is as it seems. Soon all of Hana’s hope\, her optimism\, and her d
 rive will be pushed to the limit . . .\nSisters in Yellow\nis a story of e
 nduring friendship\, deep betrayal\, and a masterpiece of teenage dreams a
 nd adult cruelties.\nJoin us on Monday\, June 22 or Tuesday\, June 23 for 
 some thrilling conversation. We hope to see you there.\nYour book club tic
 ket includes:\nA glass of wine (or another beverage of your choice).\nA th
 oughtful conversation with our brilliant moderator\, Lee Suksi.\nA $20 cre
 dit toward any book in the shop.\nGET YOUR TICKETS HERE!\nA few details:\n
 Ticket price includes HST and fees.\n$20 book credit is valid on the night
  of attendance only (non-refundable and non-transferable).\nWine is served
  on the night of the event (ID required).\nTickets are non-refundable with
 in 7 days of the event\; due to limited capacity and planning\, we’re no
 t able to transfer tickets to future book club sessions.
LOCATION:Flying Books at Neverland\, 371 Queen St. W.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR THE LAUNCH OF McSWEENEY'S QUARTERLY CONCERN
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260623T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260623T200000
UID:bm-5435320260623@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/June_23__
 _Flying_Books___784_College_St.___630pm.jpg?cb=1778726003
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nJoin Flying Books and McSweeneys for the launch
  of their Quarterly Concern #82 with contributor Jean Marc Ah-Sen reading
  from "Autofantasy\," co-authored with Paige Cooper. Light refreshments wi
 ll be served.\nThe latest issue of McSweeney’s National Magazine Award
 –winning quarterly comes to you housed inside an M-shaped\, accordion-st
 yle\, joined-at-the-back double-hardcover\, folding and unfolding with art
  by Rob Sato to reveal a world that expands when the book is open and cont
 racts when the book is closed. Inside this architectural feat of a book-ar
 t object is a stunning work of never-before-translated fiction by Alejandr
 o Zambra\; alongside dazzling new literature from Kenan Orhan\, Deb Olin U
 nferth\, and Hannah Kingsley-Ma\; letters by Halle Butler\, John Wray\, an
 d Massoud Hayoun\; an Odyssean\, toad-centric\, natural-wine-drunk novella
  by Tom Bubul\; a twelve-page\, two-book-wide fever dream of a comic by Ma
 tt Panuska that can be read only if and when both books are open at once\;
  a twenty-four-page booklet of additional drawings by Sato\; and so much m
 ore. Breathe in the sweet late-spring air\, and let this mysterious puzzle
  box of an issue envelop you.\nFeaturing fiction by:\nAlejandro Zambra (tr
 anslated by Megan McDowell)\nAndrew Roe\nAvigayl Sharp\nDeb Olin Unferth\n
 Hannah Kingsley-Ma\nJean Marc Ah-Sen\nKenan Orhan\nMatt Lapata\nPaige Coop
 er\nTom Bubul
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College Street
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR THE LAUNCH OF SOMETIMES\, FOREST BY ELEE KRALJII GARDI
 NER
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260625T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260625T200000
UID:bm-5571220260625@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/sometimes
 __forest_x_Toronto.png?cb=1780142071
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nPlease join Flying Books College and Talon Book
 s for the Toronto launch of sometimes\, forest by Vancouver Poet Laureate 
 Elee Kraljii Gardiner. The evening\, hosted by Gary Barwin\, will feature 
 a reading\, musical performance\, and interview.  Attendance is free\, b
 ut you can  RSVP\nhere.\nsometimes\, forest\nalternatively rails at and d
 esires a fluid beloved\, sometimes forest\, sometimes lover\, friend\, mot
 her\, or an absence the speaker yearns for in herself. But the coastal tem
 perate rainforest continues foresting\, existing independently of the spea
 ker’s wants or needs\, a place of both refuge and harm. Returning daily 
 to the same woods\, the speaker notices minute seasonal changes and consid
 ers her own internal changes too.\n\nMeanwhile\, fires\, heat domes\, and 
 landslides mirror hormonal heat and biological surges. Considering how net
 works of lateral support mitigate and challenge hierarchical\, individuali
 stic structures\, sometimes\, forest develops a theory of hylofeminism tha
 t attends to a deep\, communal connection with nature as a relational way 
 of being with the self and the more-than-human world.\nElee Kraljii Gardin
 er is the author of two poetry books\,\nTrauma Head (\nAnvil Press\, 2018)
  and\nserpentine loop\n(Anvil Press\, 2016). She is editor of the antholog
 ies\nAgainst Death: 35 Essays on Living\n(Anvil Press\, 2019) and\nV6A: Wr
 iting from Vancouver’s Downtown Eastside\n(Arsenal Pulp Press\, 2012) an
 d nine anthologies from Thursdays Writing Collective\, a program she found
 ed with Downtown Eastside writers. She is also author of the chapbooks\nTr
 auma Head: the medical file\n(Otter Press\, 2017) and\nResidence\n(Otter P
 ress\, 2023).\n\nOriginally from Boston\, Elee lives in Vancouver\, where 
 she directs Vancouver Manuscript Intensive\, a program pairing authors wit
 h mentors. She serves as the seventh Poet Laureate of Vancouver until the 
 conclusion of 2028 and is focusing on sound-related projects.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College Street
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:MAKE IT MAKE SENSE: THE FLYING BOOKS NONFICTION BOOKCLUB
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260629T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260629T203000
UID:bm-5542320260629@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/June_Non_
 Fiction_Book_Club_.png?cb=1779908919
DESCRIPTION:[Book Club]\nJoin Tomas Hachard\, author of\nCity in Flames\n\
 , on Monday\, June 29th for a  discussion of Leanne Betasamosake Simpson
 ’s powerful book\,\nTheory of Water.\nLeanne Betasamosake Simpson writes
  about water the way few writers write about anything — from the inside 
 out. The acclaimed Nishnaabeg writer moves through skiing\, rivers\, memor
 y\, and environmental devastation in Theory of Water\, weaving together pe
 rsonal experience\, Indigenous knowledge\, and political thought into some
 thing that feels both urgent and intimate. At its heart\, it's a book abou
 t listening — to water\, to land\, to each other. We think it will make 
 for a remarkable conversation and we hope you'll join us.\nGET YOUR TICKET
 S HERE\nYour book club ticket includes:\nA glass of wine (or another bever
 age of your choice).\nA thoughtful conversation with Tomas Hachard\, autho
 r of\nCity of Flames\nA $20 credit toward any book in the shop.
LOCATION:Flying Books at Neverland\, 371 Queen St. W.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
